panvk/kmod: a JM submission's callback keeps what it needs, and reads its command buffers only for the first failure

With our patch 0005 a GPU fault on the job manager is a lost device, and with 0006 the queue's
waits have a time limit; other paths of the tree lose the device too (a query time-out, no memory
at submit). Once the device is lost, the app's waits answer VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST, and the app can
free the command buffers and the VkEvents of submissions that report later. In the tree their
callback on the kmod's event thread reads them: the command buffers' batches and jobs
(panvk_kbase_report_faults) and the batches' event operations with their events
(panvk_signal_event_syncobjs, op->event).

This patch:
- A submission takes the sync objects of its vkCmdSetEvent and vkCmdResetEvent at submit, in
  order, and its callback signals and resets those.
- The callback reads the command buffers (the fault report, and its log line "panvk: the kbase
  submission did not complete") only while the device is not lost yet: for the first failure
  only. The blocking path and the callback for a part of a large submission (0005) log that line
  in the same way, so a fault in any part of a submission gives the line once.
- A sync object's handle carries a generation in bits 20-30, above the table's index + 1 in bits
  0-19 (so the table takes up to 524288 objects). A handle that is freed is refused (-EINVAL), also
  once its slot holds a new object: a late signal or reset of a freed object reads no freed memory
  and changes no other object. Every user of the handles in the tree keeps them as uint32_t values
  and only gives them back to the sync provider, and a handle is never 0.

--- a/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c
+++ b/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c
@@ -907,6 +907,7 @@
 
 struct ks_obj {
    uint8_t state;
+   uint16_t gen; /* the handle's high bits: one more each time the slot is freed */
    /* KS_SUBMITTED from an import: nothing here signals it, the sync file below does. */
